"""@@Surface fire spread is described@@A wildfire DSS draws on heterogeneous sources, and none of them observes the fire state directly. Weather networks and forecasts deliver gridded wind, humidity, temperature, and precipitation. Satellite and airborne imagery deliver active-fire detections and burned-area estimates. GIS layers deliver fuel models, terrain, roads, settlements, and assets. Resource tracking delivers the position and state of crews and aircraft. These sources differ in modality, coverage, refresh rate, and reliability. For this reason the inputs to the decision logic are defined as bounded, observation-derived features rather than as raw sensor fields. A quantity is admitted as a feature only if it is observable from what sensors and reports deliver, bounded so that it can be normalised to a fixed interval, meaningful to an expert without translation, and not redundant with the remaining features. Ten features satisfy these conditions and together cover threat, exposure, feasibility, and timing. Fire intensity and spread potential are the standard descriptors of how a front burns and how fast it moves {7}. Weather severity condenses the weather observations into spread and intensity potential, as fire-weather index systems do. Fuel load is the input of every surface-fire prediction {27}. Asset exposure quantifies the values at risk, and ignition proximity reflects that human-caused ignitions cluster near roads and infrastructure. Resource accessibility, access and road status, and suppression availability together determine whether suppression can be mounted and whether evacuation routes are open {31}. Temporal urgency carries the incident clock, since the value of an order decays as the fire advances {23}. The features and their sources are listed in Section IV.""",
